\noindent\textbf{Common mistake --- swapping connections.}
Putting an ammeter in parallel with a low-resistance path can create a near
short circuit and destroy the meter. Putting a voltmeter in series blocks the
current (very large $R_V$).
\end{mdframed}

\qbtext{QB\,403 PHY14031109}{%
The current flowing through a voltmeter is usually very small because\\[0.3em]
A.~the voltmeter is ideal.\\
B.~the voltmeter is ohmic.\\
C.~the voltmeter has a high resistance.\\
D.~the voltmeter has a low resistance.
}{C}{%
A voltmeter is connected in parallel, so to avoid loading the circuit it must
draw negligible current. That requires a \textbf{high} resistance
$\Rightarrow$ \textbf{C}.
``Ideal'' means infinite resistance (A is a special case of the same idea, but
the direct reason for small current is high $R$).}

\qbtext{QB\,403 PHY14031111}{%
In a circuit the internal resistance of the battery is $2\,\Omega$. $R$ is a
variable resistor. The terminal voltage across the battery is $8\,\mathrm{V}$
when the variable resistor is set at $16\,\Omega$. Find the e.m.f.\ of the battery.\\[0.3em]
A.~$8.5\,\mathrm{V}$\quad
B.~$9\,\mathrm{V}$\quad
C.~$9.5\,\mathrm{V}$\quad
D.~$10\,\mathrm{V}$
}{B}{%
Current $I=V/R=8/16=0.5\,\mathrm{A}$.
Then $\mathcal{E}=V+Ir=8+(0.5)(2)=9\,\mathrm{V}$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{B}.}

