\section{Electric power and energy}

\subsection{Power as energy per time}
Electrical power delivered to a component is
\[
P = \frac{E}{t} = VI,
\]
because energy $E=QV$ and $I=Q/t$. For an ohmic resistor $V=IR$, so
\[
P = VI = I^{2}R = \frac{V^{2}}{R}.
\]
Use whichever form matches the known quantities. Energy transferred in time $t$:
\[
E = Pt.
\]

\subsection{Which form when?}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Same current through series elements: compare $P=I^{2}R$ --- larger $R$
        dissipates more power.
  \item Same voltage across parallel elements: compare $P=V^{2}/R$ --- smaller $R$
        dissipates more power.
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
