    $P=VI$ always (instantaneous averages for d.c.)\\[0.2em]
    $P=I^{2}R$ convenient in series\\[0.2em]
    $P=V^{2}/R$ convenient in parallel};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{mdframed}[style=misbox]
\noindent\textbf{Common mistake --- brightness and ``power rating''.}
A bulb marked ``$12\,\mathrm{V}$,\,$24\,\mathrm{W}$'' dissipates $24\,\mathrm{W}$
only when operated at its rated voltage. In a network its actual power depends
on the actual p.d.\ across it.
\end{mdframed}

\qbfig{QB\,402 (Q1 on sheet)}{B}{../../../_qb-assets/QB402/p-01.png}{%
Focus on the three-bulb circuit (first question). Energies in $1\,\mathrm{s}$
are powers: $P_1=4\,\mathrm{W}$, $P_2=8\,\mathrm{W}$, $P_3=4\,\mathrm{W}$.
$B_2$ and $B_3$ are in parallel (same $V$), so $I\propto P$ gives
$I_3:I_2=1:2$ --- (1) true.
Battery energy $=4+8+4=16\,\mathrm{J}$ in $1\,\mathrm{s}$, so
battery$\,:\,B_2=16:8=2:1$ --- (3) true.
For voltages: $I_1=I_2+I_3=3I$ if $I_3=I$, $I_2=2I$.
Then $V_1=P_1/I_1=4/(3I)$ and $V_2=8/(2I)=4/I$, so $V_1:V_2=1:3$, not $1:2$.
(2) false $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{B}.}

