\subsection{Temperature and non-ohmic behaviour}
For most metals, resistivity rises with temperature: ions vibrate more, so
electrons scatter more. A filament lamp starts with lower cold resistance; as
it heats, $R$ rises and the $I$--$V$ curve bends. That is why many ``non-ohmic''
DSE graphs are really temperature effects, not a failure of $V=IR$ at a fixed
instantaneous operating point.

